WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.968
A graph describes relationships through vertices and edges

00:00:03.968 --> 00:00:10.211
These four vertices might represent places, with an edge whenever a direct connection exists

00:00:10.211 --> 00:00:16.900
The geometry of the drawing is optional; which pairs are connected is the essential information.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.537
A vertex's degree counts the edges touching it

00:00:21.537 --> 00:00:25.292
The degrees here are one, three, two, and two

00:00:25.292 --> 00:00:31.967
Adding them counts every undirected edge twice, once at each endpoint, which explains the degree-sum identity.

00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:35.223
A path follows connected edges

00:00:35.223 --> 00:00:43.047
Without weights, a shortest path minimizes the number of edges, so reaching D from A through B takes two steps

00:00:43.047 --> 00:00:48.133
A disconnected graph would not guarantee a path between every pair of vertices.

00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:51.033
Weights change the goal

00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:57.033
If the direct B-to-D edge costs four, then the two-edge route from A costs five

00:00:57.033 --> 00:01:00.233
The three-edge route through C costs only three

00:01:00.233 --> 00:01:04.233
Fewest edges and least total cost are different optimization questions.
